<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#leysingi">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leysingi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as leysingr</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In Norse law, a leysingr (plural leysingi) was a freed slave, or freed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrall">thrall</a>. A slave whose freedom had been given but not made public was a frjálsgjafar, the lowest rank of freedman, who became a leysingi by giving a freedom ale; manumission had to be confirmed by oath and announced at an assembly or in church. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">Freedmen</a> stayed inferior in the law codes: the former master controlled where they lived and whom they married, they could not leave his property without permission, and their children's inheritance was restricted. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a>, most widespread in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>, was small-scale and household-based, and it had disappeared in most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> by the 14th century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Leysingi was the Scandinavian word for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> around the twelfth century, and the book Kings and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> states that it was used as early as the ninth century in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> for the freedmen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>. The provincial law codes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> used many archaic words, often drawn from poetry, for the stages of freedom, and their meaning was not always understood. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a> frjálsgjafi usually meant the owner who gave freedom, while one set of Norwegian laws used it for the lowest grade of freed slave. The historian Ruth Mazos Karras argues that the codes sometimes use leysingi and frjálsgjafi interchangeably, the second marking a freedman who had not yet given his freedom ale.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A freedman who had not given his freedom ale could neither manage his own business nor arrange his own marriage, and even afterwards he needed his former master's permission. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> the former master held rights over the freedman's inheritance, coming before more distant kin even after the freedom ale, and in Iceland he inherited if the freedman had no children; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> could buy the right for their children to inherit. Norwegian law forbade a master to take back freedom once given and obliged him to help a freedman in need. Under Danish and Swedish law the master ceased to be involved, and the freedman was adopted into a kin group instead.</p><p>The Grágás of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a>, written down in 1117, shows how closely the law guarded these lines. A child born to a slave father and a free woman could not be a lawful heir, and a child conceived while its mother was still a slave but born after her freedom had to be freed a second time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The laws named several ways to freedom. Under Norwegian law a free father could free his child by a slave, and other relatives could free a slave child if they took responsibility for it. In Swedish law a slave falsely passed off as free in order to marry a free woman went free, and under the Uppland Law so did a Christian slave whom his master tried to sell. In the law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> the king could free a slave sent to do his master's military service.</p><p>A master or the community might free a slave for faithful service, and some slaves bought their freedom, though few could gather the wealth. The sagas show <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> holding land and wealth: in the Laxdaela saga, Hrut gave his thrall Hrolf his freedom, money and a dwelling.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leysingi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The freed slave (<b>leysingi</b>) was free but not equal.</blockquote><a href="/societies/vikings">Social Hierarchy of the Vikings</a></li></ul></section>
